<section style="padding-top:40px">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px">

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lead"><strong>Expect £12 to £15 per court per hour, and normalise the quote before you compare it.</strong> Across 18 UK venues whose published rates we read, the median was £13.45. But a centre advertising £11.60 can be dearer than one advertising £15.00, because it sells 45 minutes. Before you negotiate the rate, settle three things: what unit the centre is selling, whether ten sessions on one invoice unlocks both a discount and a VAT exemption, and whether the whole hall costs less than the courts inside it.</p>

      <p>Get this wrong and you find out in August: the renewal lands, it is up again, and the committee starts talking about putting subs up rather than asking why nobody read the council's fees paper in February.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:30px">
      <h4>The short version</h4>
      <ul style="margin:10px 0 0">
        <li><strong>The headline rate is the least comparable number you have.</strong> Slot length varies from 45 to 60 minutes, and some centres charge per player rather than per court.</li>
        <li><strong>Ten sessions is the number that matters twice.</strong> It is the threshold for HMRC's block-booking VAT exemption and, at some centres, for a commercial discount. Same number, two different savings.</li>
        <li><strong>Check the whole hall against the courts inside it.</strong> At two venues we surveyed, hiring the entire hall costs less than hiring four of its five courts.</li>
        <li><strong>The club rate is real and mostly unpublished.</strong> Where centres do publish one it runs up to a third below the casual rate. Most say "contact us", which is the point.</li>
        <li><strong>The education sector is a different market.</strong> Universities and school lettings in our sample priced below the leisure-centre band.</li>
        <li><strong>Reception cannot change a price.</strong> Rates are set annually, in public committee papers, months before they take effect.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:22px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem">Written for UK club organisers hiring courts from a leisure centre, school or university. Prices are what 22 venues published on 26 August 2026 and typically change each April. The VAT section follows HMRC's published guidance and is not tax advice.</p>

    <div class="guide-toc" style="margin-top:26px">
      <h4>In this guide</h4>
      <a href="#unit">What are you actually buying?</a>
      <a href="#what-to-pay">What should you expect to pay?</a>
      <a href="#drivers">What drives the difference?</a>
      <a href="#block">Does block booking get you a discount?</a>
      <a href="#vat">Is VAT charged on court hire?</a>
      <a href="#negotiable">What else is negotiable besides the rate?</a>
      <a href="#whole-hall">Is the whole hall cheaper than the courts inside it?</a>
      <a href="#who">Who do you talk to, and when?</a>
      <a href="#cua">Is the venue bound by a community use agreement?</a>
      <a href="#terms">Which contract terms matter as much as the rate?</a>
