    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:34px">

      <h2 id="unit">What are you actually buying?</h2>
      <p>Three centres quoting "about twelve pounds a court" can mean three different things, and the gap between them is wider than anything you will negotiate off the rate.</p>
      <p><strong>Slot length is not standard.</strong> Castle Leisure Centre in Bury sells a 45-minute court. Halton Borough Council sells 55 minutes. Most of the rest sell an hour. Nobody labels this as a discount or a premium; it just sits in the booking system, and it moves the real price by up to a third.</p>
      <p><strong>Some centres charge per player, not per court.</strong> The PEAK in Stirling prices badminton at £6.50 per adult with a minimum of two players. For a club running doubles that is roughly £26 a court-hour, against a £13.45 median for the same two hours of the same sport. A club that budgets per court and books a per-player venue has doubled its cost before anyone picks up a racket.</p>
      <p><strong>And "a court" does not always mean four people on it.</strong> Better's Brook Leisure Centre in Belfast states that the initial booking covers one player and reserves the court, with additional players paying separately at reception. Bury charges a guest fee of half the court fee for non-member additional players. Ask what the price includes before you compare it to anything.</p>

      <h2 id="what-to-pay">What should you expect to pay?</h2>
      <p>The median is £13.45 per court per hour, and half of UK venues sit between £12.00 and £15.00.</p>
      <p>No published national figure for badminton court hire exists, so we made one. On 26 August 2026 we read the published rate cards and booking pages of 22 UK venues, spread across councils, leisure trusts, universities, schools and one private operator. Eighteen of them sell a comparable per-court unit. The other four sell something else, which is a finding in its own right.</p>

      <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
        <img src="/img/diagrams/court-hire-rates.svg" alt="A chart of eighteen UK venues' adult peak badminton court prices, normalised to a sixty-minute court and sorted from cheapest to dearest. They run from nine pounds at Swansea Bay Sports Park to sixteen pounds twenty at the Eric Liddell Sports Centre in London. The median is thirteen pounds forty-five. The three cheapest are a university, a university and a school. Councils cluster between twelve pounds and fifteen pounds forty-seven." style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px" />
        <figcaption style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.92rem">Eighteen venues, adult peak rate, normalised to a 60-minute court. Read 26 August 2026. Every venue is named, and the figures re-derive from the published survey.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p style="margin-top:22px"><strong>Now the part that makes the table usable.</strong> Before you compare your quote to that median, convert it to an hour. Bury advertises £11.60 and Sefton advertises £15.00, and Bury is the more expensive of the two.</p>

      <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
        <img src="/img/diagrams/court-hire-slot-length.svg" alt="Two pairs of bars. On the left, the advertised prices: Castle Leisure Centre in Bury at eleven pounds sixty, Sefton at fifteen pounds. On the right, the same two normalised to a sixty-minute court: Bury rises to fifteen pounds forty-seven because it sells forty-five minutes, overtaking Sefton, which stays at fifteen pounds because it already sells an hour. The order reverses." style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px" />
        <figcaption style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.92rem">The same two centres, before and after normalising. Bury sells 45 minutes, so its cheaper-looking price is the dearer one.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p style="margin-top:22px">Divide the price by the minutes and multiply by sixty. It takes ten seconds and it is the only way two quotes mean the same thing.</p>
      <p>One caveat we will not hide: one of our eighteen rows is an upper bound rather than a price. Botwell Green publishes "up to £12.70" rather than a figure. Leaving it out moves the median to £13.65 and changes neither quartile, so the shape of the answer holds either way.</p>

      <h2 id="drivers">What drives the difference?</h2>
      <p>Operator type is the signal you can see; region is not separable from it at any sample a club could reasonably gather.</p>
      <p>The education sector prices below the leisure-centre market. The three cheapest venues in our survey were Swansea Bay Sports Park at £9.00, Newcastle University at £10.00 and Plympton Academy's hall at £10.50. Councils clustered higher, with a median of £13.65 across nine venues, and the most expensive venue in the sample was the one private company in it.</p>
      <p>We are not going to tell you how much of that is region, because our own data cannot support the claim. All three of our North West venues are council-run, the only Welsh venue is a university, and the only private operator is in London. Operator type and geography are tangled together, and any percentage we attached to either would be invented. Directionally: look at who runs the building before you look at where it is.</p>

      <h2 id="block">Does block booking get you a discount?</h2>
      <p>Where a centre publishes a club or block rate, the discount is large - and most centres do not publish one.</p>
      <p>Halton Borough Council publishes badminton club hire at £9.30 for a 55-minute court against £14.00 for the same court booked casually. Normalised, that is £10.15 an hour against £15.27: a third off, for being a club.</p>
      <p><strong>Watch for the trap in how block rates are listed.</strong> Tendring District Council's published block rate is £14.40, which is higher than its casual rate of £13.65. The 20% discount for ten or more sessions on one invoice is applied separately, bringing it to £11.52 - about 16% below casual. A club comparing headline numbers would conclude that block booking costs more.</p>
      <p>Everywhere else, the club rate exists and is withheld. North Yorkshire says there are "reduced rates for block and club bookings" without a figure. Codsall and Wombourne both say to email. Better's Belfast centres say block bookings are by enquiry only. That is not an oversight, and it is the best news in this guide: a price that is never published is a price that is set in a conversation.</p>

      <h2 id="vat">Is VAT charged on court hire?</h2>
      <p>A single casual booking is normally standard-rated at 20%, and a series of ten or more sessions can be exempt - which is why ten sessions is the most valuable number in this guide.</p>
      <p>HMRC's guidance on land and property treats letting facilities for playing sport as normally standard-rated. It then sets out an exemption for a series of lets, and the conditions are specific enough to be worth reading against your own booking. All of them must be met: ten or more sessions; the same sport each time; the same place; an interval between sessions of at least one day and no more than fourteen; the series paid for as a whole with written evidence; the hirer a school, club or association; and exclusive use during each session.</p>
      <p><strong>Two of those conditions bite in practice.</strong> The fourteen-day interval means a fortnightly club is inside the rule but a club that skips a fortnight for half-term may break its own series. And "paid for as a whole" carries a real cost: the evidence has to show payment is due whether or not each session is used. You are buying the exemption with your cancellation flexibility, and that trade is worth making consciously rather than discovering later.</p>
      <p><strong>Now the part every article about this gets wrong, including by omission.</strong> Qualifying for the exemption does not mean 20% comes off your invoice. It depends entirely on who you are hiring from:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>A commercial operator</strong> charging you standard-rated hire: a qualifying series should remove the VAT, and you should see it.</li>
        <li><strong>A local authority:</strong> probably nothing changes, because since 2023 HMRC has accepted that councils' own leisure services can be treated as outside the scope of VAT altogether. There may be no 20% there to remove. You are already not paying it.</li>
        <li><strong>A charitable leisure trust:</strong> it depends on whether the trust qualifies as an eligible body under the sports exemption, which turns on its constitution rather than on your booking.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>So do not walk in asking for 20% off. Ask two questions instead: <em>is our booking standard-rated at the moment</em>, and <em>if we structure it as a series of ten or more sessions on one invoice, does that change the VAT treatment?</em> Those are answerable by the person who raises your invoice, and they cost nothing to ask.</p>
      <p>That this works is not theoretical. Sefton's published fees schedule codes badminton court hire with an exemption that applies to not-for-profit groups "or when the block booking exemption can be applied", and standard rating otherwise. Tendring codes casual hire and block bookings differently from each other. Two councils, independently, publishing the rule in their own price lists.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h4>Worked example: a club at Wombourne, 4 courts, 2 hours, 38 weeks</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">Wombourne Leisure Centre publishes £12.50 a court and £59.00 for the whole five-court hall.</p>
        <ul style="margin:12px 0 0">
          <li>Four courts for two hours: <strong>4 &times; 2 &times; £12.50 = £100.00 a week</strong>, or £3,800 over a 38-week season.</li>
          <li>The whole hall for two hours: <strong>£59.00 &times; 2 = £118.00 a week</strong>, or £4,484 a season.</li>
          <li>So the fifth court, plus exclusive use of the hall, costs <strong>£18.00 a week</strong> - £9.00 per extra court-hour against a £12.50 list rate.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top:12px">And the VAT? Wombourne is run by a district council, so the supply is likely already outside the scope of VAT. There is probably no 20% to save here, whatever your booking looks like. That is the honest answer for a large share of UK clubs, and it is why the question to ask is about your own supplier rather than about the rule.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="negotiable">What else is negotiable besides the rate?</h2>
      <p>More than the rate, and we are going to be straight with you about the evidence here: no operator we could find publishes its block-booking terms, so what follows is a list of things worth asking for rather than a description of what is normal.</p>
      <p>Ask about which courts and which slot, because a fixed allocation each week is worth more to a club than a floating one. Ask about storage, because a cupboard for posts, nets and a first-aid kit removes a job somebody currently does every week. Ask about access and setup time, and whether your two hours includes putting the posts out. Ask whether you can run a one-off tournament on your usual night without being charged as a new booking. Ask what happens to your slot in the school holidays.</p>
      <p>None of these appear on a rate card, all of them are decided by a person, and several of them are worth more over a season than a fifty-pence reduction in the hourly rate.</p>

      <h2 id="whole-hall">Is the whole hall cheaper than the courts inside it?</h2>
      <p>Sometimes it is cheaper outright, and it takes one line of arithmetic to find out.</p>
      <p>Multiply the per-court rate by the number of courts in the hall, and compare it to the whole-hall price. In our survey:</p>
      <div style="overflow-x:auto;margin-top:16px">
        <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.95rem">
          <thead>
            <tr style="text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--line)">
              <th style="padding:8px 10px">Venue</th><th style="padding:8px 10px">Courts &times; rate</th><th style="padding:8px 10px">Whole hall</th><th style="padding:8px 10px">Verdict</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)"><td style="padding:8px 10px">Codsall</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">5 &times; £12.50 = £62.50</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£59.00</td><td style="padding:8px 10px"><strong>Hall is cheaper</strong></td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)"><td style="padding:8px 10px">Wombourne</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">5 &times; £12.50 = £62.50</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£59.00</td><td style="padding:8px 10px"><strong>Hall is cheaper</strong></td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)"><td style="padding:8px 10px">Newcastle University</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">8 &times; £10.00 = £80.00</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£80.00</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">Exactly level</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)"><td style="padding:8px 10px">Plympton Academy</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">4 &times; £10.50 = £42.00</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£42.00</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">Exactly level</td></tr>
            <tr><td style="padding:8px 10px">Eric Liddell, London</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">5 &times; £16.20 = £81.00</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£79.20</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">Break-even just under 5</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:18px">Two of those five make the whole hall cheaper than four of its courts, and two more make the extra courts free. Exclusive use is worth having on its own terms: no stray five-a-side in the next bay, no queue of pay-and-play players at the door, and the option to spread out when forty people turn up instead of thirty.</p>

      <h2 id="who">Who do you talk to, and when?</h2>
      <p>Not reception, and not in April.</p>
      <p>Centres route block enquiries away from the booking desk. LeisurePlex in Lisburn directs block bookings to a named administrator. Codsall and Wombourne both direct them to the centre's email rather than its phone line. The pattern is consistent enough to plan around, though we should be clear this next part is our read rather than something anyone publishes: the person who answers the booking line has a price list and no authority to depart from it. Asking them for a club rate reliably produces the casual rate.</p>
      <p><strong>The timing is more useful than the routing.</strong> Council leisure prices are set annually, with effect from 1 April, and they go through committee as published papers. Sefton's 2026/27 sports fees appeared as a committee document showing the current charge, the proposed charge and the percentage increase, before any of it took effect. Oxford publishes the same thing. Halton's schedule is captioned "subject to approval".</p>
      <p>So next year's price is a public document, months before your renewal letter. Search your council's website for its fees and charges paper, find the sports pages, and you will know what is coming and roughly when the number is still amendable. Very few clubs do this, and it is free.</p>

      <h2 id="cua">Is the venue bound by a community use agreement?</h2>
      <p>If you hire a school or academy sports hall built or extended in the last twenty years or so, there may be a contractual ceiling on what it can charge you - and you can go and look.</p>
      <p>Sport England publishes a model community use agreement, and it contains a pricing clause requiring that prices are no greater than those at similar local-authority-run facilities in the area. The template also covers the split between term-time and holiday community use, an advance booking arrangement reviewed annually, identified priority groups, and a review committee that assesses usage and pricing.</p>
      <p>These agreements are typically secured by planning condition, because community use is often the mitigation offered when a development affects playing fields, and Sport England is a statutory consultee on that. Which means the agreement is a planning document: findable in your local authority's planning portal against the school's application.</p>
      <p>We cannot tell you whether your venue has one. That is a per-venue lookup and it is the reader's to do. But no other guide on this subject will tell you the lookup exists, and a club that finds one has a very different conversation from a club that opens with "could you do it a bit cheaper".</p>

      <h2 id="terms">Which contract terms matter as much as the rate?</h2>
      <p>Cancellation, variation and notice - and this is the one question in this guide where the honest answer is that nobody publishes the standard.</p>
      <p>We went looking for what a normal leisure-centre block-booking agreement contains and found nothing public at any operator. So rather than invent a norm, here is what to get in writing before you commit to a series:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>What notice must <em>they</em> give to cancel or move a session, and what happens to sessions already paid for.</li>
        <li>What notice must <em>you</em> give, and how many sessions you may cancel without losing the block rate. Remember the VAT trade: a series that qualifies for exemption is one you have agreed to pay for whether or not you use it.</li>
        <li>Whether the rate is fixed for the term or reviewed on 1 April regardless.</li>
        <li>What happens when the hall is taken for an exam, a polling station, or a school production.</li>
        <li>Whether your allocation carries over automatically at renewal, or is re-offered.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>That last one matters more than clubs expect. A block that has to be reapplied for each year is a block you can lose to whoever asks first.</p>

      <h2 id="ready">What do you need ready before you ask?</h2>
      <p>Evidence of public liability cover, and a named person who can sign.</p>
      <p>Most centres will want to see that the club is insured before letting a regular block, and for most UK clubs that cover comes through national or county affiliation rather than a separate policy. What we would flag, because it catches clubs out, is that club affiliation and individual player membership are different things and cover different people. Do not assume that affiliating the club covers every individual who turns up on a Tuesday. Check what your own affiliation actually includes, in its current wording, before you tell a centre you are covered.</p>
      <p>Beyond insurance: a constitution, a bank account in the club's name, and a named signatory. A centre letting a regular block to an unincorporated group wants to know who it is contracting with.</p>

      <h2 id="walk-away">When should you walk away?</h2>
      <p>When there is somewhere else to go - and for a lot of clubs, there is not.</p>
      <p>Start with the honest version. Most clubs have one hall that is the right size, in the right place, at the right time of the evening. If that is you, "walk away" is not a lever, and pretending otherwise wastes your time. What you have instead is the block-structure work above, which is real and does not require an alternative.</p>
      <p>But if you have not looked, look, because the education sector is a different market and it is more accessible than clubs assume. School lettings are published by the hour through letting platforms - several hundred UK schools list badminton facilities - and in our survey the school hall was cheaper per court than every council venue. Universities were cheaper again.</p>
      <p><strong>To compare a whole-hall price against your per-court rate, you need the court count.</strong> Sport England's design guidance gives the standard sizes: a four-court hall is around 33 by 18 metres, six courts 33.75 by 27, eight courts 37 by 33.75. A listing that gives dimensions but not court numbers can be converted. A listing that gives neither cannot, and we would not guess.</p>
      <p>What you give up moving to a school is usually parking, changing rooms, a bar, and the caretaker's goodwill on the night somebody is ill. Those are worth something. Price the move, then decide.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h4>If you only do one thing</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">Work out what your centre charges per <em>hour</em> before you compare it to anything. A 45-minute court at £11.60 costs more than a 60-minute court at £15.00.</p>
      </div>

